---
import Concept from '../../layouts/Concept.astro';
import Mermaid from '../../components/Mermaid.astro';

const chart = `stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> Inactive
  Inactive --> Placed: use telepipe in field
  Placed --> Placed: place a new one (old is replaced)
  Placed --> Inactive: quest start / end, title return, area change
  Placed --> Inactive: return to field via the city-side pipe

  Inactive: Inactive — no pipe
  Placed: Placed — one active pipe
`;
---

<Concept title="Telepipe">
  <div class="diagram"><Mermaid chart={chart} /></div>

  <p>A telepipe is a portable shortcut between the field and the city. The <code>TelepipeManager</code> autoload tracks it, and the cardinal rule is: <strong>at most one telepipe exists at a time</strong> — placing a new one immediately closes the old.</p>

  <h2>Round trip — the key behavior</h2>
  <p>Dropping a telepipe does <em>not</em> consume it. The player can bounce between field and city repeatedly while it stays open:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a telepipe in the field → a pipe spawns where the player stands, and a matching one appears in the city. The pipe is now <strong>active</strong>.</li>
    <li>Step into the field-side pipe → travel to the city. The pipe <strong>stays active</strong>.</li>
    <li>Go back to the field via the <strong>city teleporter</strong> (same area) → the pipe is <strong>still active</strong>, ready to take the player back to the city again. This can repeat indefinitely.</li>
    <li>The pipe only closes when the player returns to the field by stepping into the <strong>city-side pipe</strong> — that's the one-shot return that consumes it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So "returning to the field" keeps the pipe alive if done through the teleporter, but consumes it if done through the city pipe itself.</p>

  <h2>Cleared automatically when</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
    <tr><td>A new telepipe is placed</td><td><code>replaced</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>The player returns to the field via the city-side pipe (round trip done)</td><td><code>returned</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A quest is <strong>started</strong> (accepted)</td><td><code>quest_accepted</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A quest <strong>ends</strong> (completed or aborted)</td><td><code>quest_ended</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>The player returns to the <strong>title screen</strong></td><td><code>title_return</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A fresh expedition starts in a <strong>different area</strong></td><td><code>area_changed</code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>End-of-quest telepipe</h2>
  <p>A quest often spawns a telepipe at the end as a convenient ride back to the city. Because only one pipe can exist, this <strong>story telepipe replaces any pipe the player had already placed</strong>. Conversely, if the player ignores the story telepipe and drops their own, that new one closes the story telepipe in turn — whichever was placed most recently wins.</p>

  <h2>Persistence</h2>
  <p>State is in-memory only. There are no special cases for cutscenes or scripted beats — any of the events above wipe it, and an app close obviously does too.</p>

  <h2>API</h2>
  <p><code>is_active()</code>, <code>get_state()</code>, <code>place(area, section, cell, world_pos, scene)</code>, <code>cancel(reason)</code>, <code>consume_return()</code>, <code>matches_field(area, section, cell)</code>. Signals: <code>placed(state)</code>, <code>canceled(reason)</code>.</p>

  <h2>Known issues (#239)</h2>
  <p>Two bugs are open in 0.30.17: placing a new telepipe doesn't reliably close the old one (the one-active rule isn't holding), and going free-field → telepipe → city then talking to the quest counter locks the player into "Cancel Quest", stranding them on whatever free field they were on.</p>
</Concept>
